1. { -KEY- } Kb5 $1 ( { There was nothing wrong with the obvious } 1. Rh7 { but White has seen that in the pawn ending arising after the text, his king will be able to enter decisively via the back rank. } ) 
Kxe7 ( { Or } 1... Rd6 2. Rh7 e5 3. Kc5 $1 { with an easy win - Socko. } ) 
2. Kxa6 Kf7 ( { After } 2... Ke8 3. Kb6 $1 { (now Black is lacking the f6-square for his king) } Kd7 4. Kb7 Kd6 5. Kc8 e5 6. Kd8 { or } 6. Kb7 { White wins. } ) 
3. Kb7 $1 { Distant opposition. } Kg8 4. Kc8 Kg7 5. Kc7 $1 Kg8 6. Kd6 { At this moment Black resigned; let's make a few more moves: } Kf7 7. Kd7 Kf6 8. Ke8 $1 { White finally manages to bring his king behind the enemy pawns. } e5 9. Kd7 ( { There follows } 10. Kd6 { with an easy win - Socko. } ) 
 ( { By the way, another good option is } 9. Kf8 { continuing the King raid along g8-h7, in order to capture the h6-pawn, as noticed by Benner. } ) 

