 { I have a pleasant memory of the king raid idea' - it once helped me to win a last-round game, and indirectly the whole tournament. Despite various errors from both sides I find it rather instructive. This endgame should be drawn, but White keeps some practical chances due to Black's slightly compromised pawn structure. } 1. { -KEY- } Kh3 { Diagonal opposition, which helps White take the direct opposition the next move. } Kg5 2. Kg3 Kf5 $2 { Interestingly, this natural move loses the game, something that just shows the complexity of pawn endings. } ( { Correct was } 2... f5 $1 { gaining some space; e.g. } 3. Kf3 Kg6 4. Kf4 Kf6 5. g3 { (the fact that White already has to push this pawn is the key to the draw) } Ke6 6. Kg5 Ke5 7. Kg6 Ke6 $1 8. Kh5 f4 $1 { (a sacrifice on the theme of obstruction) } 9. gxf4 Kf5 10. Kh6 Kxf4 11. Kg6 Ke5 12. Kf7 Kd4 13. Ke6 Kc3 14. Kd6 Kxb3 15. Kxc5 Kxa4 16. Kb6 Kb4 17. c5 a4 { with a draw. } ) 
3. Kf3 Ke5 4. Kg4 { At this point I had my plan ready: bring the king behind the f-pawn, swap the kingside pawns, move over the king to the queenside and capture on c5 before Black captures on b3. Even so, I erred on my next move. } f5+ ( { After } 4... Kd4 $2 5. Kf5 Kc3 6. Kxf6 Kxb3 7. g4 { only White will queen. } ) 
 ( { As for } 4... Ke4 { White wins in similar style as in the main line, starting with } 5. Kh5 ) 
5. Kh5 $2 { During the game I thought this move was clever, since Black can't oppose with his king either on f5 or on d5, but in fact it throws away half a point! } ( { Obviously, } 5. Kg5 { was useless due to } f4 6. Kg4 Ke4 { and Black holds by opposition. } ) 
 ( { But by } 5. Kh4 $1 { White could have avoided Black's drawing trick on move 6 in the main line, with quite an easy win; e.g. } Kf6 ( { or } 5. Kh5 Kf4 6. Kh5 Kg3 7. Kg5 ) 
6. Kh5 Ke6 7. Kg5 Ke5 8. g3 Ke6 9. Kg6 Ke5 10. Kf7 { as later happens in the main line. } ) 
Kf6 6. Kh6 Ke5 $2 { Black returns the favour. } ( { By } 6... f4 $1 { he could have temporarily obstructed the enemy king, while preparing the same pawn sacrifice that we saw in the 2...f5 line; e.g. } 7. Kh5 Kf5 8. Kh4 f3 $1 9. gxf3 Kf4 10. Kh5 Kxf3 11. Kg5 Ke4 12. Kf6 Kd4 13. Ke6 Kc3 { and we have in fact transposed to 2...f5 above. } ) 
 ( { Even } 6... Kf7 $5 { worked; e.g. } 7. g3 Kf6 8. Kh5 f4 $1 9. gxf4 Kf5 { and we already know the idea. } ) 
7. Kg6 Ke6 8. g3 $1 Ke5 9. Kf7 { White finally manages to get behind the enemy pawns with his king, and the rest is easy. } Kd4 10. Kf6 Kc3 11. Kxf5 Kxb3 12. g4 Kxc4 13. g5 Kb3 14. g6 c4 15. g7 c3 16. g8=Q+ Kb2 17. Qg7 
 1-0

