 { Many years later, I came across a similar endgame in one of my students' games. Black has just made a huge mistake by playing ... g6-g5? instead of the correct ...f6-f5. White could now have decided the game in her favour by applying a king raid. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { This advance doesn't just gain the opposition for White, it also gains space which makes the f-pawn very dangerous in any passed pawn race. } ( { In contrast, the game went } 1. fxg5 $2 hxg5 2. h3 Ke5 3. Ke3 Kd5 { and a draw was agreed. } ) 
Kd6 ( { Slightly more tenacious than } 1... Ke5 $6 2. Kc4 Kd6 3. Kb5 Kd5 4. h3 { when we have already reached the position after move 8 in the main line. } ) 
2. Kc4 Kc6 3. h3 $1 { Again White takes the opposition. } Kd6 ( { Or } 3... Kb6 4. Kd5 Kb5 5. Ke6 Kb4 6. Kd6 $1 ( { more clear-cut than } 5... Kd6 5. Kxf6 ) 
Kb5 7. Kd5 Kb6 ( { if } 7... Kb4 { then } 8. Kc6 ) 
8. Ke6 { and the f-pawn is faster. } ) 
4. Kb5 Kd5 5. Kb6 Kd6 6. Ka6 $1 { Thus White gains the opposition two moves later, exploiting the fact that 6...Ke6 isn't available for Black. } Kc6 7. Ka5 Kd5 ( { Black could have tried } 7... c4 $5 { in similar spirit to 6...f4 of the previous example, though after } 8. bxc4 Kc5 9. Ka6 Kxc4 10. Kb6 Kd5 11. Kc7 Ke4 12. Kd6 { followed by Ke6, she would be lost anyway. Compared to the previous game, here White is heavily favoured by having the potential passed pawn on the fifth rank (f5) instead of the fourth (c4). } ) 
8. Kb5 { Now the white king's entry at c7 can't be prevented. } Kd6 9. Kb6 Kd5 10. Kc7 Ke4 11. Kc6 $1 ( 11. Kd6 { also works, in similar fashion. } ) 
Kd4 12. Kd6 { White wins. } 
