 { The king raid idea can be used in other types of endings as well, and not only by the attacking side. Here are some examples. In such bishop endings, if the weaker side can't put the king in front of the pawn, then its best place is usually behind it, vis-à-vis the enemy king. } 1... { -KEY- } Kf4 $1 { Preparing a king raid. } ( { Passive play with this piece would lead to defeat; e.g. } 1... Kf5 $6 2. Kd5 Kg6 $2 ( { it is not yet too late for } 2... Kf4 ) 
3. b5 Kf7 4. Kc6 Ke6 5. Bd4 Ba5 6. Bc5 { followed by Bd6-c7. } ) 
 ( { But the similar } 1... Kf3 { was also possible, as noticed by hong-xu. } ) 
2. b5 Ke4 3. Bd4 Bc7 4. Kc5 Kd3 $1 5. Kc6 Kc4 6. Bb6 Bf4 { Due to the correct location of the black king, White can't build a 'bridge' on c7 without losing the pawn. Had White managed to bring his king to a6, then Black would have been obliged to transfer his king to a4, thus preventing a bridge on a5. } 7. Ba7 Bc7 $1 
 1/2-1/2

