 { Here is a similar case, where Black is again in trouble due to his remotely placed king. } 1... { -KEY- } Kf6 $1 { Preparing a king raid is the only chance for salvation. } ( { Instead, the game went } 1... Kf5 $2 2. Rf7 $1 Kg6 3. Rf4 Ra5 4. Kc6 Ra6 5. Kb5 { and White later won. } ) 
2. Kxc4 Ke5 3. Kc5 ( { Or } 3. Rc6 Rb8 $1 { (preventing Kb5) } 4. Kc5 Ke4 { with similar play - Dvoretsky. } ) 
Ke4 $1 { The key move, which prepares Kd3 in order to tie the enemy pieces to the defence of the pawn. } 4. c4 Kd3 5. Rd7+ ( { After } 5. Kd5 Rd8 ) 
 ( { or } 5. Kb5 Rb8 { Black holds. } ) 
Kc3 6. Kd5 Kb4 7. c5 ( { Or } 7. Rb7 Ka5 8. c5 Ka6 { followed by ...Rh8, with similar play. } ) 
Kb5 8. c6 Rh8 $1 { The rook shifts to the long side of the pawn, thus enabling a side attack. (We will study such positions quite thoroughly in Chapter Three.) Black defends - Dvoretsky; } ( { e.g. } 8... Rh8 9. Rb7 Ka6 10. Rb1 Rh5 11. Ke6 Rh6 12. Kd7 Rh7 { etc. } ) 

